    "result_text": "I've implemented `find_candidates` in `app/catalog.py`. It makes a single Hub listing request that does the filtering, sorting and limiting on the Hub side:\n\n```python\napi.list_models(search=name_fragment, pipeline_tag=task, sort=\"downloads\", limit=limit)\n```\n\nIt returns the model ids, most downloaded first. Both tests pass: the existing one and a new one in `tests/test_catalog.py`. The new test uses a fake client, so it only checks that the right arguments are sent in one request. I haven't run it against the live Hub.\n\n- **`search`, not `model_name`:** huggingface-hub 2.0.0 (the pinned version) removed the `model_name` argument, so name matching uses `search`.\n- **Sort order:** there's no ascending/descending option, and the Hub sorts by downloads from highest down. `org_models` relies on the same behaviour.\n- **Extra `api` argument:** I added an optional `api` argument at the end, matching the other functions in the module, so the test can pass in a fake client. Calling `find_candidates(name_fragment, task, limit=5)` works as before.\n- **Name matching includes the owner:** `search` looks for the fragment anywhere in the full id, which includes the owner's name. So `\"whisper\"` also matches a model whose owner is named something like `whisper-labs`, even if the model's own name doesn't contain it."
